<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serdica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sardica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serdica was the ancient and medieval city on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Named after the Thracian Serdi, it became a Roman city and the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> Mediterranea, and hosted a church council in 343. The Bulgarian khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> captured it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 809, and it was later known by the Slavonic name Sredets before taking the name Sofia.</p></div>
